<!--
  BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.PseudoCode
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/schema/block"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/schema/block https://blockml.org/schemas/core/block.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:lang="org.blockml.bml.language">
  <name>PseudoCode</name>
  <type>doc:PseudoCode</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A guide to PseudoCode semantics</is>

  <documentation>
    PseudoCode (org.blockml.bml.language.PseudoCode) is a ProgrammingLanguage for free-form
    pseudocode Capability implementations. The code must be internally consistent but is
    otherwise unconstrained — an LLM may interpret it.

    Why PseudoCode exists:
    • BlockML describes semantics, not a single target language
    • Capability implementations need human- and LLM-readable bodies before renderers map them
    • PseudoCode avoids premature binding to Java, TypeScript, SCAD, or Python syntax
    • Semantic descriptions in PseudoCode stay stable across Worlds and render targets

    When to use PseudoCode:
    • Documenting intended behaviour on Capabilities during design
    • Framework and domain blocks where the semantic contract matters more than executable code
    • Blocks read by LLMs for code generation — PseudoCode is the preferred semantic layer

    When to use concrete languages:
    • org.blockml.bml.language.TypeScript, OpenSCAD, Python, … when a target renderer transpiles
      or emits the implementation directly
    • Block definitions may restrict permitted languages via the languages aggregation

    Prefer describing semantics in Capability implementation bodies (PseudoCode or natural
    language blocks like English/German) rather than encoding behaviour in compiler special cases.
    The renderer interprets; BlockML declares intent.
  </documentation>

  <references>
